Helps verify integrity and ensure password confidentiality

A hash collision is when two inputs create the same output.
This is attempted to be avoided, but since possible inputs are infinite and possible outputs are limited, this is hard
Hashes give a fixed length output, which means that their only 2^(numberOfBits) possible outputs. 
This means collisions are inevitable, but a good algorithm takes steps to ensure the risk of them is negligable

Do not trust either MD5 or SHA1 since they've both fallen susceptible to hash collision attacks

