Rainbow tables trade time-to-crack a hask for disk space. They're a table of known passwords and their corresponding hashes.

websites CrackStation and Hashes.com employ massive rainbow tables for craching passwords that are not salted

salt is a randomly generated value stores in the database and should be unique to each user.

Salts don't need to be kept private

hash functions like Bcrypt and Scrypt handle this automatically

/etc/shadow is where linux stores its password hashes
  File contains 9 fields, the first two of which are the login name and the encrypted password
  more information about the other fields can be found with the command:
man 5 shadow
The 'Encrypted Password' field contains the hashed passphrase iwth 4 components:
    prefix (algorithm ID)
    options (parameters)
    salt
    hash
it's saved in the format:
$prefix$options$salt$hash

Prefixes:
$y$ yescrypt
$gy$ gost-yescrypt
$7$ scrypt
$2b$/$2y$/$2a$/$2x$ bcrypt based on Blowfish
$6$ sha512crypt
$md5 sunMD5
$1$ md5crypt

